          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Pierre\",\"addressee\":\"Hélène\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Je vous aime!\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Pierre's love for Hélène as true. Its truth-presenting force remains clear even though he delivers it as a conventional formula and feels ashamed of how feeble it sounds. His immediately preceding inner statement confirms that this is the state he means to avow.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"«Теперь уж поздно, всё кончено; да и я люблю ее», подумал Пьер.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"слова эти прозвучали так бедно, что ему стало стыдно за себя\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.82,\"reason\":\"In this occurrence, the declaration conventionally affirms Pierre's newly established position as Hélène's fiancé and his acceptance of the marital bond. It does not create the engagement by itself or specify a fresh promise: the family blessing, the couple's conduct, and Pierre's treatment of her as his fiancée have already established that undertaking. Nevertheless, his recollection that these are the words required 'in such cases' gives the expression some ritual ratifying force rather than making it a wholly separate emotional report.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Пьер молча держал руку своей невесты\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"«Всё это так должно было быть и не могло быть иначе, — думал Пьер, — поэтому нечего спрашивать, хорошо ли это или дурно? Хорошо, потому что определенно, и нет прежнего мучительного сомнения».\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"— Je vous aime! — сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The words are not represented as an involuntary or reflex-like emotional discharge. Pierre pauses, searches for the conventional thing to say, and then deliberately produces the formula; the exclamation mark does not alter that characterization.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"«Что-то такое особенное говорят в этих случаях», думал он, но никак не мог вспомнить, чтò такое именно говорят в этих случаях.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"— Je vous aime! — сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"No substantial residual force is needed beyond the direct avowal and its moderate, context-dependent ratification of the engagement. Its courtship-formula aspect is already captured by P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Pierre and Hélène have been left alone after their families have treated them as newly engaged and blessed the union. Following Hélène's kiss, Pierre regards the matter as settled, recalls the conventional words expected on such an occasion, and says the focal expression directly to her.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The contextual undertaking is Pierre's acceptance and affirmation of being Hélène's fiancé, with marriage as the understood relationship, although no detailed terms are stated. The focal declaration ratifies that position rather than independently making a new promise. Hélène's kiss and the family's blessing precede the declaration and show acceptance of the engagement; the supplied scene gives no distinct verbal response from her after the focal words.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Leo Tolstoy's War and Peace, a work with which I have prior familiarity; the classification here relies only on the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports the represented avowal and provides direct evidence that Pierre believes he loves Hélène at that moment. It also depicts fear, shame, sexual attraction, and social pressure, so it does not establish the precise quality or durability of that affection. No post-utterance verbal uptake by Hélène is supplied.\"}"
